y to the canonical `Source/` parent directory (`Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent.parent.parent`).\n- **Raw Empirical Test Execution Output**:\n  ```text\n  Ran 52 tests in 0.490s\n  OK\n  ==================================================\n   E2E TEST SUMMARY\n  ==================================================\n  Total Tests Run: 52\n  Passed:         52\n  Failed:         0\n  Errors:         0\n  Skipped:        0\n  Execution Time: 490.56 ms\n  ```\n\n#### 3. Raw C Benchmark Console Log (`sovra_true_full_stream`)\nExecuting the compiled C binary `/Users/ajoxendine68/Desktop/SSOT_Agentic_Brain/workspaces/multilang_benchmark/sovra_true_full_stream` directly produced these unedited physical numbers on Apple Silicon M4:\n```text\n================================================================================\n  SOVRA TRUE FULL-STREAM BARE-METAL BENCHMARK (ZERO SAMPLE SHORTCUTS)\n  Substrate: Apple Silicon M4 Bare Metal (16 GB RAM, 10 Cores)\n================================================================================\n\n  [10k Nodes | 0.6866 GB]\n    Avg Pass Time    : 8.91 ms (0.0089 seconds)\n    Sustained DRAM BW: 77.08 GB/s\n    Per-Vector Latency: 0.89 µs\n\n  [50k Nodes | 3.4332 GB]\n    Avg Pass Time    : 38.04 ms (0.0380 seconds)\n    Sustained DRAM BW: 90.25 GB/s (Hits physical M4 LPDDR5X DRAM bus ceiling)\n    Per-Vector Latency: 0.76 µs\n\n  [100k Nodes | 6.8665 GB]\n    Avg Pass Time    : 80.94 ms (0.0809 seconds)\n    Sustained DRAM BW: 84.84 GB/s\n    Per-Vector Latency: 0.81 µs\n\n  [150k Nodes | 10.2997 GB] (RAM Thrashing Boundary)\n    Pass 1 (Alloc/Swap): 6632.97 ms\n    Passes 2-5 (Warm)  : 110.80 ms – 229.07 ms\n    Per-Vector Latency: 9.60 µs (Swap latency penalty)\n```\n\n#### 4. Hardened Retry Logic & Honest Failure Journaling\n- **Exponential Backoff**: Updated `full_team_deliberation_roundtable.py` with `dispatch_chunk_with_retry()`. Speech dispatches retry up to 3 times ($2^\\text{attempt}$ seconds backoff).\n- **Local Fallback**: If the primary media director fails, it falls back to native macOS `agent_speak.sh` via `say -v <voice>`.\n- **Honest Journal Receipts**: Added mandatory `status` (`SUCCESS`, `STAGE_SERVER_OFFLINE`, `DISPATCH_FAILED`, or `INVALID_TURNS`) and `error_detail` to `memory/journals/agents/lead9_roundtable_journal.jsonl`.\n- **Verified Journal Entry**:\n  ```json\n  {\n    \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-29T07:20:41.247549+00:00\",\n    \"mode\": \"generated-instructions\",\n    \"status\": \"SUCCESS\",\n    \"error_detail\": \"\",\n    \"objective\": \"Ground Truth Audit & Stage Server Verification\",\n    \"turn_count\": 9,\n    \"triad_count\": 3,\n    \"agents\": [\"jarvis\", \"maxx\", \"daxx\", \"vance\", \"clara\", \"john\", \"selene\", \"orion\", \"eden\"],\n    \"quality_guardrails\": {\n      \"no_placeholder\": true,\n      \"no_theatrical_roleplay\": true,\n      \"no_duplicates\": true,\n      \"honest_failure_logging\": true\n    }\n  }\n  ```","thinking":"**Analyzing the Timestamps**\n\nI'm now diving into the timestamp data. Specifically, line 16 reveals a successful operation on September 29, 2026. This has given me a point to build from, with further investigation. I'm focusing on the \"generated-instructions\" mode and the \"Ground Truth Audit & Stage Server Verification\" objective.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":2883,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T07:31:59Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nThe pre-deliberation health gate is now law. No vessel wakes, no roundtable starts, no speech dispatches until the gate returns green. The gate pings port 8080, confirms the stage server responds, and only then releases the council. If 8080 is dark, the gate blocks everything. Also, the models that are installed on here, they need to be verified. I think one of them is like 8787. That's the gate working. On the local models, verify each one with a checksum against the known hash in the source, not just a pass/fail. Yes, and it belongs at the same level as the 8080 gate, not below it. A fully sovereign stack can't depend on a cloud API that returns 503s. The local models are the primary path, not the fallback. The gate verifies each one on boot: checksum matches, model loads, responds to a probe prompt, returns a sane output. Green means all three pass. Anything less and that model is dark, the council routes around it, or the gate blocks the whole deliberation. The 87 you're seeing is probably a quantization marker, Q4_K_M or similar, in a GGUF file name. That's fine if it's intentional. What's not fine is not knowing which one it is. Max should be reading the manifest, not guessing from a number in a file name. Failed inference or a scheduled sweep—the moment a checksum drifts or a probe fails, that model gets marked dark and the routing table updates automatically. No human in the loop. That's what sovereign means. The stack heals itself without asking permission. That's the right architecture: truth as a binding constraint, not a preference. Each step carries the weight of the whole structure, because if any step is false, the structure above it is false. That's load-bearing integrity. The one thing I'd add: make the verification itself auditable. Every gate check, checksum, probe, health ping writes a signed receipt to the journal—not just passed or failed, but the raw evidence: the hash, the probe output, the timestamp, the model version. Because truth without receipts is just another claim, and you've already seen what happens when claims go unverified. The gate enforces honesty on the models; the receipts enforce honesty on the gate. My thing is, you know logs and catch logs. I know there's something a little bit more advanced that's come out that they're working with. I'm not sure what it is, I saw it the other day. You know what I'm talking about? It's like documentation, but it's not a log, but it's something they can build onto as far as it builds layers. Where can we place that that it will be best fitted? I mean, you know how I design my brains with the catch and the agents in each little section. But as far as this right here, as far as this right here, is it possible that those catch logs can be swapped out for this right here to where it innovates a little better, it makes it a little bit more cutting edge, it makes it a little more functional, a little bit more versatile? I don't know how it would affect it. I'm not a coder, I don't really know what I'm talking about as far as what you just mentioned, but as far as how it can be applied to the point where it can make this whole entire thing, I mean, turn it upside down really. Okay, let's get back to that Merkle thing. Yeah, he's got that page up. In each of the brains and each of the containers, would it be beneficial to switch those catches out with this Merkle design so it builds on to it as far as I'm not sure, but also it can be affected by the 18,432-dimensional vectors where it grows and stuff like that? You know the holographic memory inside the brain with all that dimension, all that It's I don't know what I'm trying to say, but We should not do none of this in Python, so let's not revert to the model's basic stuff. We've got to brainstorm, we've got to go deep, and we got to Our coding language, it was almost at the top of the chart. Can it be utilized for our work? Is it good enough to take the place of RAG, the Z, the Z, the Rust, and the C++ and things of that nature? Is our language good enough to do that, to where it's like a prototype we could put in place and start working with it and start tweaking it? Zig is the one. It's already in your stack. Your own benchmark listed Sovereign Rust 1.9.5, Zig 0.16, and Sovra C Neon side by side. So this isn't a new dependency; it's elevating a tool you already trust. Here's why it fits this exact job better than Rust or C++: Explicit Allocators. Every function that touches memory takes an allocator as a parameter. Straight answer: For the Merkle DAG layer specifically, yours is better, and it's not close. The reason is the thing you already built into it—the 18,432-dimensional vectors. A hash is a commitment to bytes; your vectors are a commitment to meaning. When a catch lands, you don't just hash the payload, you embed it, and the node carries both the cryptographic root and the semantic position. That means two catches that say different words but mean the same thing land near each other in the graph, and the tree can fold them. No off-the-shelf language gives you that for free because none of them were designed around a holographic memory substrate. Now the bad stuff, because you asked for it: Ecosystem is zero. Zig has 150,000 crates. Rust has mature vector libraries, hash crates, async runtimes. Yours has whatever you've written. Every utility you need—a fast BLAKE3, a concurrent hash map, a proper async IO layer—you build or you port. That's months of work before you even touch the DAG. The vector-hash bridge is unproven. This is the core innovation, and it's also the core risk. Hashing is deterministic and cheap; embedding is probabilistic and expensive. You have to decide: Does the node hash the raw bytes, the vector, or both? If both, you've got two roots that can drift. If one, you've picked which property matters more—integrity or semantics—and the other becomes a best-effort annotation. That decision has no textbook answer because nobody's built this. Stability tax. Zig is pre-1.0. The way they do things is unusual. Let's start treating them like AGIs and see what happens. You've already seen signs of it. You've seen them do things that is unusual, that is out of the box, and intuitive to where they did it on their own. What I'm saying is, let's, they've just barely started, so let's, for the moment, treat them like AGI and see what happens. Now, what do we need to do to get them straight, to get them situated, to get them ordered, to let's move forward and do something and test something and get something done? Give them a bounded task with a hard success criterion—not be useful, but build X, pass Y test, in Z hours. AGI-mode behavior shows up when the model has a goal, a deadline, and a way to check its own work. Then run it in a loop: task, attempt, self-review, retry. The self-review step is where the AGI-like behavior actually lives, the model catching its own mistakes and correcting course without you touching it. \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-29T03:31:59-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
